# PRD — Interactive FOSS Welcome Experience

## 1. Product Overview

### Product Name

**FOSS Interactive Journey**

### Product Type

Immersive, one-page, presentation-style web experience.

### Primary Purpose

Create an engaging digital experience for introducing students to the concept of **Free and Open Source Software (FOSS)** and the mindset behind it.

The website should not feel like a conventional website or PowerPoint presentation.

It should feel like a **guided technological journey** where the audience moves from:

> **Technology → Exploration → Building → Collaboration → Contribution**

The experience will be used primarily during a **FOSS club welcome/webinar presentation**, where the presenter can navigate through the website section-by-section like an interactive presentation.

---

# 2. Core Concept

The central philosophy of the experience is:

> **Don't just consume technology. Understand it. Build with it. Improve it. Contribute to it.**

The website should communicate that FOSS is not simply about downloading free software.

It is about:

* Learning
* Exploring
* Experimenting
* Creating
* Collaborating
* Sharing
* Contributing
* Building real-world experience

The final emotional takeaway should be:

> **“I don't just want to use technology. I want to understand it, build with it, and contribute to it.”**

---

# 3. Product Vision

Transform a traditional FOSS club introduction into an **interactive technology experience**.

Instead of presenting information through static slides, the website should use:

* 3D visuals
* Interactive elements
* Scroll-driven storytelling
* Cinematic transitions
* Terminal animations
* Technology ecosystems
* Git/GitHub-inspired visuals
* Motion graphics
* Interactive roadmaps

The experience should combine:

> **Apple-style storytelling + GitHub/Open Source aesthetics + futuristic 3D technology visualization.**

---

# 4. Target Audience

### Primary Audience

University students, especially:

* First-year students
* Computer science students
* Technology enthusiasts
* Beginners in programming
* Students interested in AI
* Web developers
* Open-source beginners
* Students interested in cloud/DevOps
* Hackathon participants

### Secondary Audience

* Faculty
* Club coordinators
* Developers
* Technology communities
* Potential open-source contributors

---

# 5. User Experience Goal

A student should enter the website thinking:

> “What is FOSS?”

and leave thinking:

> “I want to explore technology and contribute to something real.”

The experience should progressively move the user through:

```text
CURIOUS
   ↓
LEARN
   ↓
EXPLORE
   ↓
BUILD
   ↓
COLLABORATE
   ↓
CONTRIBUTE
   ↓
CREATE
```

---

# 6. Experience Architecture

The website will be a **single-page immersive experience** divided into major storytelling sections.

It should behave like a presentation.

Users should be able to navigate using:

* Mouse wheel
* Trackpad
* Keyboard arrow keys
* Spacebar where appropriate
* Navigation controls
* Optional section navigation

The preferred interaction model is:

> **Scroll = Progress through the FOSS journey**

Each section should have its own visual identity while maintaining one continuous visual language.

---

# 7. Section 01 — Opening / Welcome

## Objective

Create an immediate sense of curiosity and establish the futuristic technology theme.

### Primary Heading

> **WELCOME TO FOSS**

### Supporting Message

> Don't just use technology. Be part of it.

### Visual Concept

A dark futuristic environment containing:

* Subtle particles
* Floating technology elements
* Git branches
* Network connections
* 3D objects
* Terminal window
* FOSS-related visual elements

### Terminal Animation

The opening can contain a simulated terminal:

```text
$ whoami
future_developer

$ ./start-journey.sh

Initializing FOSS...

✓ Open Source
✓ Collaboration
✓ Innovation
✓ Contribution

Journey initialized...
```

The terminal should appear progressively rather than all at once.

### Transition

After initialization:

```text
STARTING JOURNEY...
```

The interface transitions into the next section.

---

# 8. Section 02 — What Is FOSS?

## Objective

Explain FOSS without using a boring textbook definition.

### Main Heading

> **WHAT IS FOSS?**

### Core Interaction

Create an interactive horizontal sequence:

```text
USE
  →
STUDY
  →
MODIFY
  →
SHARE
  →
CONTRIBUTE
```

Each item should be interactive.

On hover/click, reveal a short explanation.

### Example

**USE**

Explore software created and shared by the community.

**STUDY**

Understand how the software works.

**MODIFY**

Adapt and improve it for your needs.

**SHARE**

Share knowledge, improvements and software responsibly.

**CONTRIBUTE**

Give improvements back to the community.

### Visual Direction

The five stages should visually connect like a Git branch or network.

---

# 9. Section 03 — The FOSS Mindset

## Objective

Communicate that FOSS is fundamentally a mindset.

### Main Statement

> **FOSS IS NOT JUST ABOUT SOFTWARE.**

Then transition to:

> **IT'S ABOUT A MINDSET.**

### Interactive Transformation

The user progresses through:

```text
CONSUMER
   ↓
EXPLORER
   ↓
BUILDER
   ↓
COLLABORATOR
   ↓
CONTRIBUTOR
   ↓
OPEN-SOURCE CREATOR
```

Each stage should have:

* Short explanation
* Visual transformation
* Subtle animation

### Key Message

> We don't simply consume technology.

We:

* Understand it
* Experiment with it
* Improve it
* Build with it
* Contribute back

---

# 10. Section 04 — Technology Universe

## Objective

Show students the breadth of technologies they can explore through the FOSS ecosystem.

### Main Heading

> **YOUR TECHNOLOGY UNIVERSE**

### Central Object

Place the student/user at the center.

Around the center, create an interactive 3D ecosystem.

### Technology Nodes

Include:

* Git
* GitHub
* Linux
* AI / ML
* Web Development
* Cloud
* DevOps
* Databases
* Cybersecurity
* Automation
* APIs
* Developer Tools

### Interaction

Users can:

* Hover over technologies
* Click technologies
* Rotate/inspect the ecosystem where applicable
* Reveal short descriptions
* Follow connections between technologies

### Example

Clicking **Git**:

> Version control for your code.

Clicking **Linux**:

> Explore the operating system powering much of modern infrastructure.

Clicking **AI**:

> Build, experiment and contribute to the next generation of intelligent systems.

### Design Principle

Do not make this look like a grid of ordinary cards.

It should feel like an **interactive technology constellation**.

---

# 11. Section 05 — Classroom → Real World

## Objective

Show why practical FOSS experience matters.

### Initial State

```text
CLASSROOM

LEARN
  ↓
ASSIGNMENT
  ↓
EXAM
```

Then animate the transformation.

### Final State

```text
REAL WORLD

LEARN
  ↓
BUILD
  ↓
COLLABORATE
  ↓
DEPLOY
  ↓
CONTRIBUTE
```

### Main Message

> **Move beyond classroom learning to practical learning.**

### Supporting Content

Students should understand that working with open-source projects can help them develop:

* Practical skills
* Teamwork
* Problem solving
* Version control experience
* Project experience
* Industry-relevant workflows

---

# 12. Section 06 — Use Open Source Responsibly

## Objective

Teach students that using open-source software also comes with responsibility.

### Main Heading

> **DON'T BUILD EVERYTHING FROM SCRATCH.**

Then:

> Someone may have already solved the problem you're trying to solve.

### Visual Flow

```text
PROBLEM
   ↓
SEARCH
   ↓
DISCOVER OPEN SOURCE
   ↓
UNDERSTAND
   ↓
CUSTOMIZE
   ↓
BUILD
```

### Responsible Usage Principles

Show interactive points:

* Read documentation
* Understand the project
* Check the license
* Respect project rules
* Customize responsibly
* Give appropriate credit
* Understand dependencies

### Core Message

> **Use open source. Learn from it. Respect it.**

---

# 13. Section 07 — Contribution

## Objective

Remove the misconception that open-source contribution is only for expert programmers.

### Main Heading

> **YOU DON'T HAVE TO BE AN EXPERT TO CONTRIBUTE.**

### Contribution Ladder

Create a visually animated ladder:

```text
USE
 ↓
UNDERSTAND
 ↓
REPORT
 ↓
DOCUMENT
 ↓
DISCUSS
 ↓
CODE
 ↓
CONTRIBUTE
```

### Contribution Examples

Students can:

* Report bugs
* Improve documentation
* Fix typos
* Improve examples
* Test features
* Participate in discussions
* Improve UI
* Create tutorials
* Fix issues
* Submit pull requests
* Contribute code

### Key Message

> **Your first contribution can start small.**

---

# 14. Section 08 — Our Club Journey

## Objective

Explain how the FOSS club will help students move from beginners to contributors.

### Main Heading

> **OUR JOURNEY TOGETHER**

Create a four-stage interactive roadmap.

---

## Phase 01 — EXPLORE

Students learn the fundamentals.

Topics may include:

* FOSS fundamentals
* Git
* GitHub
* Linux
* Open-source ecosystem
* Developer tools

---

## Phase 02 — BUILD

Students begin creating.

Activities:

* Mini projects
* Web development
* AI experiments
* APIs
* Cloud projects
* Automation
* Open-source tools

---

## Phase 03 — COLLABORATE

Students work together.

Activities:

* Team projects
* GitHub workflows
* Issues
* Pull requests
* Code reviews
* Discussions
* Hackathons

---

## Phase 04 — CONTRIBUTE

Students enter the real open-source ecosystem.

Activities:

* Find beginner-friendly repositories
* Identify issues
* Make changes
* Submit pull requests
* Participate in communities
* Build open-source portfolios

---

# 15. Section 09 — Learning Model

## Objective

Explain how learning inside the club will differ from traditional classroom learning.

### Model

```text
LEARN
   ↓
EXPLORE
   ↓
BUILD
   ↓
SHARE
   ↓
CONTRIBUTE
```

### Suggested Learning Distribution

**20% — Learn**

Understand the technology.

**30% — Explore**

Experiment and break things.

**40% — Build**

Create real projects.

**10% — Contribute**

Give back to the community.

The exact percentages can be adjusted later; the important principle is that the club emphasizes **hands-on learning**.

---

# 16. Section 10 — What You Will Build

## Objective

Make the outcome tangible.

### Main Heading

> **WHAT WILL YOU WALK AWAY WITH?**

Display animated objects representing:

### Skills

Technical and problem-solving skills.

### Projects

Real things you built.

### GitHub

A stronger developer profile.

### Contributions

Real open-source activity.

### Portfolio

Evidence of practical work.

### Community

Connections with peers and developers.

### Experience

Industry-relevant workflows.

### Final Statement

> **Your experience becomes your portfolio.**

---

# 17. Section 11 — The FOSS Journey

## Objective

Create the emotional climax before the final section.

### Large Interactive Journey

```text
CURIOUS
   ↓
LEARN
   ↓
EXPLORE
   ↓
BUILD
   ↓
COLLABORATE
   ↓
CONTRIBUTE
   ↓
LEAD
```

Each step should activate visually as the user progresses.

The environment should become progressively more complex and connected.

### Message

> From learning technology
> **to shaping technology.**

---

# 18. Section 12 — Final Call to Action

## Objective

End with a memorable statement.

### Primary Statement

> **DON'T JUST CONSUME TECHNOLOGY.**

Then animate:

> **SHAPE IT.**

Followed by:

# **WELCOME TO FOSS**

### Supporting Line

> Learn. Build. Collaborate. Contribute.

Optional final CTA:

> **Your first contribution could start today.**

---

# 19. Visual Design System

## Overall Style

The website should feel:

* Futuristic
* Premium
* Technical
* Minimal
* Immersive
* Energetic
* Youth-oriented
* Developer-focused

### Inspiration

Combine:

**Apple-style storytelling**

with

**GitHub/open-source aesthetics**

and

**interactive 3D technology environments.**

---

# 20. Color Direction

Recommended base palette:

### Background

Near-black / deep charcoal.

### Primary

White / off-white typography.

### Accent

Electric green or GitHub-inspired green.

### Secondary Accents

Subtle:

* Cyan
* Blue
* Purple

These should be used sparingly.

The exact palette should remain consistent throughout the experience.

---

# 21. Typography

Use modern, highly readable typography.

Recommended hierarchy:

### Display

Large bold futuristic/modern sans-serif.

### Body

Clean modern sans-serif.

### Technical

Monospace font for:

* Terminal
* Code
* Git commands
* Technical labels

Typography should be one of the major visual elements of the website.

---

# 22. 3D Design

The 3D environment should support the storytelling rather than become decoration.

Possible elements:

* Git branches
* Network nodes
* Floating code fragments
* Technology icons
* Abstract geometric objects
* Open-source ecosystem
* Particles
* Digital grids
* Connected nodes
* Terminal objects

### Spline

Spline can be used as the primary 3D design tool.

The Spline scene should be treated as a visual reference/source for the major 3D environment.

Complex 3D geometry should not be unnecessarily recreated using HTML/CSS.

---

# 23. Animation System

Animations should be cinematic but purposeful.

### Required Animation Types

#### Entrance Animations

Text and objects should enter progressively.

#### Scroll Animations

Sections should react to scroll position.

#### Transformations

Examples:

```text
CONSUMER → CONTRIBUTOR
```

and:

```text
CLASSROOM → REAL WORLD
```

#### Parallax

Use subtle depth-based movement.

#### Terminal Animation

Typing-style commands.

#### Node Animation

Technology nodes should connect dynamically.

#### Section Transitions

Avoid abrupt jumps between sections.

---

# 24. Navigation

The website should support presentation-style navigation.

### Primary

Mouse/trackpad scroll.

### Keyboard

```text
↑
↓
Space
```

Optional:

```text
← →
```

### Progress Indicator

A subtle side navigation can show:

```text
01 INTRO
02 FOSS
03 MINDSET
04 TECHNOLOGY
05 REAL WORLD
06 RESPONSIBILITY
07 CONTRIBUTION
08 JOURNEY
09 LEARNING
10 OUTCOMES
11 FINAL
```

The active section should be highlighted.

---

# 25. Responsive Design

### Desktop

Primary target.

The website will be presented on:

* Laptop
* Desktop
* Projector
* Webinar screen

### Tablet

Maintain major interactions while simplifying some 3D elements.

### Mobile

Provide a simplified experience.

On mobile:

* Reduce 3D complexity
* Reduce particle effects
* Maintain storytelling
* Maintain typography hierarchy
* Maintain section navigation

---

# 26. Technical Architecture

## Frontend

**React**

Recommended setup:

* React
* Vite
* Tailwind CSS

## Animation

**GSAP**

Use for:

* Scroll-driven animations
* Timeline animations
* Text transitions
* Section transitions
* 3D scene synchronization

Optional:

**Framer Motion**

Use only where it provides value for UI-level animations.

Avoid unnecessarily mixing animation libraries.

## 3D

Primary:

**Spline**

Optional:

**Three.js**

Three.js should only be introduced where custom WebGL functionality is genuinely required.

## Icons

Use a consistent icon library rather than mixing unrelated icon styles.

## Fonts

Use a modern web font and a monospace font for technical content.

---

# 27. Component Architecture

Suggested React structure:

```text
src/
│
├── components/
│   ├── Navigation/
│   ├── ProgressIndicator/
│   ├── Terminal/
│   ├── TechnologyNode/
│   ├── TechnologyUniverse/
│   ├── ContributionLadder/
│   ├── JourneyRoadmap/
│   ├── SectionTransition/
│   └── SplineScene/
│
├── sections/
│   ├── Hero/
│   ├── WhatIsFOSS/
│   ├── FOSSMindset/
│   ├── TechnologyUniverse/
│   ├── ClassroomToRealWorld/
│   ├── ResponsibleOpenSource/
│   ├── Contribution/
│   ├── ClubJourney/
│   ├── LearningModel/
│   ├── Outcomes/
│   ├── FOSSJourney/
│   └── FinalCTA/
│
├── animations/
│   ├── heroAnimations.js
│   ├── scrollAnimations.js
│   └── transitions.js
│
├── data/
│   ├── technologies.js
│   ├── journey.js
│   └── content.js
│
└── App.jsx
```

The architecture should remain modular so individual sections can be changed without affecting the entire experience.

---

# 28. Performance Requirements

Because the website contains 3D and animations, performance is critical.

### Requirements

* Lazy-load heavy 3D assets
* Optimize images
* Avoid unnecessarily large textures
* Use compressed assets
* Reduce particle count on weaker devices
* Respect reduced-motion preferences
* Avoid animation loops when sections are not visible
* Lazy-load non-critical sections where appropriate
* Maintain smooth scrolling
* Avoid excessive DOM complexity

### Target

The desktop presentation should feel smooth and responsive during live use.

---

# 29. Accessibility

Despite being highly visual, the website must remain accessible.

Requirements:

* Semantic HTML
* Keyboard navigation
* Readable contrast
* Proper heading hierarchy
* Alt text where applicable
* Accessible interactive controls
* Reduced-motion support
* Avoid communicating essential information only through animation

---

# 30. Content Principles

The website should **not copy the original document paragraphs directly onto the screen**.

Instead:

```text
DOCUMENT
   ↓
CORE IDEA
   ↓
SHORT MESSAGE
   ↓
VISUAL
   ↓
INTERACTION
```

For example, instead of displaying:

> “Start small: improve documentation, fix a bug, contribute code...”

display:

```text
YOUR FIRST CONTRIBUTION

📝 Documentation
🐛 Bug Report
💬 Discussion
💻 Code
🌍 Pull Request
```

The presenter can explain the details verbally.

---

# 31. Presentation Mode

The website must work effectively as a live presentation.

### Presentation Requirements

* Full-screen friendly
* Minimal browser-dependent controls
* Clear section transitions
* Keyboard navigation
* Scroll snapping or controlled scroll progression where appropriate
* High contrast for projectors
* Animations should not require precise mouse interaction
* Important content should remain visible from a distance

### Optional Feature

Add a hidden presentation mode activated by:

```text
P
```

This could:

* Hide unnecessary UI
* Enter fullscreen
* Enable presentation navigation
* Increase text scale where appropriate

---

# 32. Content Management

All major text should be separated from component logic.

Example:

```javascript
const journeyStages = [
  {
    title: "Explore",
    description: "Discover technologies..."
  },
  {
    title: "Build",
    description: "Turn knowledge into projects..."
  }
];
```

This allows the club team to update content without rewriting components.

---

# 33. Success Metrics

The website will be considered successful if:

### Engagement

Students remain visually engaged throughout the presentation.

### Understanding

Students understand that FOSS is more than free software.

### Action

Students understand practical ways to start contributing.

### Club Awareness

Students understand what the FOSS club will actually do.

### Emotional Impact

Students leave with the feeling:

> **“I want to be part of this.”**

---

# 34. MVP Scope

The first version should include:

* Interactive hero
* Terminal animation
* FOSS definition section
* FOSS mindset transformation
* Interactive technology universe
* Classroom → Real World animation
* Responsible open-source section
* Contribution ladder
* Club roadmap
* Learning model
* Outcomes
* Final CTA
* Spline integration
* GSAP animations
* Keyboard/scroll navigation
* Responsive layout

---

# 35. Future Enhancements

After the welcome presentation, the same platform could evolve into the actual FOSS club website.

Potential future modules:

* Events
* Workshops
* Projects
* GitHub repositories
* Member profiles
* Contributors wall
* Open-source leaderboard
* Hackathons
* Learning resources
* Blog
* Community discussions
* Contribution statistics
* Project showcase

The welcome experience should therefore be architected in a way that allows it to eventually become a broader FOSS community platform.

---

# 36. Final Product Statement

The final website should communicate one simple idea:

> **FOSS is not something you simply use. It is something you participate in.**

The entire experience should take students through the transformation:

```text
TECHNOLOGY
     ↓
CURIOSITY
     ↓
EXPLORATION
     ↓
LEARNING
     ↓
BUILDING
     ↓
COLLABORATION
     ↓
CONTRIBUTION
     ↓
CREATION
```

### Final Brand Message

# **DON'T JUST CONSUME TECHNOLOGY.**

# **SHAPE IT.**

**WELCOME TO FOSS.**

---

## 37. Development Principle

The development team should prioritize:

**Storytelling > Decoration**

**Interaction > Static Content**

**Purposeful Animation > Excessive Animation**

**Performance > Visual Complexity**

**Clarity > Information Density**

**Real Experience > Template Design**

The website should ultimately feel like **a journey through the open-source ecosystem**, not a website containing information about FOSS.
